Destroy the previous tunnel when a re-registration replaces it.

update_contact_ipsec_params() only reuses the contact's existing SPI/port pair
when ipsec_reuse_server_port is on AND the new CK/IK match the stored ones.
A re-registration challenged with a fresh AKA vector fails that test, so
acquire_spi() hands out a new pair and create_ipsec_tunnel() installs a second
tunnel. Nothing removes the first one: ipsec_create() replaces the contact's
ipsec_t and frees the struct, but the kernel state it described stays, and its
pair is never released back to the pool.

What survives is specifically the old inbound SAs and the old policies. The
outbound SAs are keyed by dst=UE plus the UE's own spi_uc/spi_us, which a UE
that keeps its Security-Client SPIs across re-registrations does not change, so
the new tunnel simply overwrites those states. The inbound ones are keyed by the
P-CSCF's spi_pc/spi_ps, which just changed — so they are a distinct pair of
states that nothing refers to any more. At contact expiry destroy_ipsec_tunnel()
tears down whatever the contact currently points at, and the orphan is left
installed for good. Every re-challenge therefore leaks two SAs, four policies
and one slot out of ipsec_max_connections.

The teardown has to happen before create_ipsec_tunnel(), not after: as above the
two tunnels share the UE's SPIs, so removing the old one afterwards would delete
the outbound states the new one had just installed. Guarded on the stored
parameters being a real, different tunnel — a first registration has the
placeholder ipsec_t from save_pending(), whose spi_pc/spi_ps/port_pc/port_ps are
all zero, and destroying that would remove the outbound SAs by the UE's SPIs
while leaving nothing else to clean up.
diff --git a/src/modules/ims_ipsec_pcscf/cmd.c b/src/modules/ims_ipsec_pcscf/cmd.c
index 8904799..861f101 100644
--- a/src/modules/ims_ipsec_pcscf/cmd.c
+++ b/src/modules/ims_ipsec_pcscf/cmd.c
@@ -862,6 +862,7 @@ int ipsec_create(struct sip_msg *m, udomain_t *d, int _cflags)
 	security_t *req_sec_params = NULL;
 	ipsec_t *s = NULL;
 	ipsec_t *old_s = NULL;
+	ipsec_t *old_ipsec = NULL;
 	// set to 1 once req_sec_params->data.ipsec is handed to the contact (initial
 	// registration); if it stays 0 that ipsec_t was only used to build the
 	// tunnel and must be freed before returning, otherwise it leaks
@@ -949,6 +950,28 @@ int ipsec_create(struct sip_msg *m, udomain_t *d, int _cflags)
 		goto cleanup;
 	}
 
+	// If the contact already had a tunnel and update_contact_ipsec_params()
+	// did not reuse its parameters - a re-registration whose CK/IK changed
+	// takes a fresh pair from acquire_spi() - that tunnel is still installed in
+	// the kernel and its port pair is still marked used. Tear it down here,
+	// before the replacement is created: the old and the new one share the UE's
+	// SPIs, so their outbound SAs are the same kernel states and removing them
+	// afterwards would take the new tunnel's with them.
+	old_ipsec = pcontact->security_temp->data.ipsec;
+	if(old_ipsec && old_ipsec != s && old_ipsec->spi_pc && old_ipsec->spi_ps
+			&& old_ipsec->port_pc && old_ipsec->port_ps
+			&& (old_ipsec->spi_pc != s->spi_pc
+					|| old_ipsec->spi_ps != s->spi_ps
+					|| old_ipsec->port_pc != s->port_pc
+					|| old_ipsec->port_ps != s->port_ps)) {
+		LM_DBG("Replacing tunnel on contact [%.*s]: destroying the previous one "
+			   "(client port %d, server port %d, spi_pc %u, spi_ps %u)\n",
+				ci.aor.len, ci.aor.s, old_ipsec->port_pc, old_ipsec->port_ps,
+				old_ipsec->spi_pc, old_ipsec->spi_ps);
+		destroy_ipsec_tunnel(
+				ci.received_host, old_ipsec, pcontact->contact_port);
+	}
+
 	fun_ret_c = create_ipsec_tunnel(&req->rcv.src_ip, s);
 	if(fun_ret_c != 0) {
 		LM_ERR("IPSEC tunnel creation failed.\n");
@@ -956,7 +979,7 @@ int ipsec_create(struct sip_msg *m, udomain_t *d, int _cflags)
 	}
 
 	if(req_sec_params != NULL) {
-		ipsec_t *old_ipsec = pcontact->security_temp->data.ipsec;
+		old_ipsec = pcontact->security_temp->data.ipsec;
 		pcontact->security_temp->data.ipsec = s;
 		// s is now owned by the contact (freed at contact expiry by
 		// free_security()); do not free it on the cleanup path
